;; Docs for samizdat/markdown.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "markdown.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}, and a literal
;; at sign is @"@".

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Markdown in a samizdat document: the commands markdown and include-markdown.")
  (see markdown include-markdown markdown->pieces)
  (example """
    @markdown|{
    ## Why

    Because *some* things are quicker to write this way.[^1] See |@ref["why"].

    - [x] tables, task lists, ~~strikethrough~~ and footnotes, as GitHub has them

    [^1]: And some are not.
    }|

    @include-markdown["post.md"]
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{(text markdown)} reads the Markdown: CommonMark, with the
    extensions of GitHub Flavored Markdown. This module translates the
    result into the tree of samizdat. After the translation, no Markdown
    is left, and each converter sees the same tree as for samizdat markup:

    @itemlist{
    @item{A heading is a @code{Heading}, with an id that @code{@"@"toc} and @code{@"@"ref} know.}
    @item{A footnote is a note of the document, numbered with the other notes.}
    @item{A table is a @code{TableBlock}, with one head row and one group.}
    @item{Raw HTML is a @code{RawBlock} or a @code{RawInline} for the format @code{"html"}.}
    @item{A code block has the first word of its info string as its language.}
    @item{The typography pass runs over the text, as over other text.}
    }

    The translation leaves out what samizdat does not have: the title of a
    link, and the number that an ordered list starts at.

    The commands are in @code{standard-commands}.
    """))

(:doc markdown
  (summary "The command markdown: its body is Markdown, translated into the document.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "The blocks of the Markdown, as pieces.")
  (see include-markdown markdown->pieces)
  (example """
    @markdown|{
    A paragraph with *emphasis*, and |@em{a command} in it.
    }|
    """)
  (reference """
    Write the body as @code{|{...}|} when the Markdown has braces or
    @code{@"@"} characters in it. @code{{...}} works too, when its braces
    balance.

    A command works in the body: @code{@"@"em{x}} in a @code{{...}} body,
    and @code{|@"@"em{x}} in a @code{|{...}|} body. Each command is
    expanded where Markdown puts it. In text, it is what the command
    makes. In code, a link target or raw HTML, it is the text of what the
    command makes. A command that makes a block must be alone in a
    paragraph.

    The command is rejected when it has an argument, a keyword or no
    body, and when a command that makes a block stands in running text.
    """))

(:doc include-markdown
  (summary "The command include-markdown: a Markdown file, translated into the document.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "The blocks of the Markdown, as pieces.")
  (see markdown markdown->pieces)
  (example """
    @include-markdown["notes/post.md"]
    """)
  (reference """
    The argument is the path of the file. The path is relative to the
    file that the command is in. The file is added to the @code{sources}
    of the document. The file is read as it
    is: an @code{@"@"} in it is a character, not a command. YAML front
    matter at the start of the file is left out.

    The command is rejected when it has a keyword or a body, when it has
    no path, and when the file does not exist.
    """))

(:doc markdown->pieces
  (summary "Markdown text as pieces of the document, for a command of your own.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The command that calls it. A rejection names this command.")
  (arg text "The Markdown.")
  (returns "The blocks of the Markdown, as pieces. The footnotes are added to the notes of the document.")
  (see markdown include-markdown)
  (example """
    ;; @readme: the README.md of the project, as part of the document.
    (: readme Command)
    (defun (readme cx c)
      (markdown->pieces cx c (read-relative cx c "README.md")))
    """)
  (reference """
    The text is Markdown only. An @code{@"@"} in it is a character.
    """))
